triage: fix_tests: The game offered take_2 as a legal move, so row 2 was open in the game's own state. The test expected take_2 to be refused and row 2 to keep its card, which only makes sense if the test marked row 2 closed while it still held a card. Under the rules that state cannot happen: a row closes only when it is taken, and taking moves all its cards into the taker's collection, so a closed row is always empty until the cycle ends. The test also depends on an internal open/closed flag whose name the interface does not fix.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: The game offers only legal actions: place_1 on the full row is not in seat 1's offer, so the scripted illegal move could not be played. The move actually recorded was take_2, and the game scored it correctly (one red card plus the Favourite bonus is 7). The test's expected 12 assumes row 1 was taken, which the recorded move never asked for.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_game: The rules state plainly that after a PLACE the next active player is the next seat in order that has not taken this cycle, and that this can be the same player again. The engine can express this. In the test, seat 1 placed while seats 2 and 3 had already taken, and the game never offered seat 1 the take of the row it was filling, so the next-active logic is wrong.
